.Dd $Mdocdate: September 7 2026 $
.Dt OPENBAR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openbar
.Nd lightweight status bar for cwm and other X11 window managers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 1
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Op Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a status bar designed for
.Xr cwm 1
and other X11 window managers.
It displays system information including CPU speed and temperature,
free memory, load average, battery status, public and private
IP addresses, and WireGuard VPN status.
.Pp
Its visual style, colour palette and configuration syntax are
modelled after
.Xr cwm 1
so that the bar blends in as if it were part of the window manager.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 1
Run one update cycle and exit.
.It Fl c Ar path
Use the configuration file at
.Ar path
instead of the default.
.It Fl h
Print a usage message and exit.
.El
.Pp
Configuration is loaded from, in order:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the file passed with
.Fl c
.Pq which must exist
.It
.Pa ~/.openbarrc
if it exists and is readable
.It
.Pa /etc/openbarrc
.El
.Pp
See
.Xr openbarrc 5
for the configuration format.
.Sh PROCESS ARCHITECTURE
.Nm
runs as one or two processes on
.Ox :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Main process
Reads configuration, manages the X11 window, and queries kernel
interfaces for CPU, memory, load, battery, host name and network
interface statistics.
.It Network worker
When public network information is enabled, a child process is forked
before sandboxing.
It is the only process retaining
.Dq inet
and
.Dq dns ;
it fetches the public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses from
.Pa https://ifconfig.me
over HTTPS using
.Xr tls_init 3 ,
validates the responses with
.Xr inet_pton 3 ,
and reports them to the main process through a bounded
.Xr socketpair 2
protocol.
The worker has no access to X11, the configuration file, battery or
sensor devices, or any other parent resource.
.El
.Sh WIDGETS
The following widgets can be enabled with
.Ic show :
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It hostname
System host name.
.It date
Current date and time, updated at minute boundaries.
.It cpu
CPU clock speed and temperature.
The clock speed is read from
.Xr sysctl 2
.Va hw.cpuspeed
once at startup, before
.Xr pledge 2 ,
because that interface is not pledge-readable; the temperature
.Va ( hw.sensors )
is refreshed every five seconds.
When no CPU temperature sensor exists, an
.Sq x
is shown instead.
.It mem
Free physical memory in MB, refreshed every two seconds.
.It load
1-minute load average, refreshed every two seconds.
.It bat
Battery charge percentage, refreshed every thirty seconds.
The segment switches to the urgent colour when the charge is at or
below 15%.
.It net
Public IPv4, public IPv6 and the private IPv4 address of the
configured interface.
The public addresses are refreshed at most every five minutes; the
private address every ten seconds.
.It vpn
WireGuard status, refreshed every ten seconds.
A tunnel counts as connected when a
.Sy wg*
interface is up, running and has an assigned address.
.El
.Pp
All widgets are disabled by default.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.openbarrc
Per-user configuration.
.It Pa /etc/openbarrc
System-wide configuration.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Ss Sandboxing
Both processes use
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
on
.Ox
to limit permitted system calls and filesystem visibility.
.Ss Main process
Filesystem visibility is locked to:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix
.Pq read-write, X11 socket
.It
.Ev XAUTHORITY
or
.Pa ~/.Xauthority
.Pq read-only, when the file exists
.It
.Pa /dev/apm
.Pq read-only, only if the battery widget is enabled and the node exists
.It
the fontconfig directories
.Pa /etc/fonts ,
.Pa /var/cache/fontconfig ,
.Pa /usr/X11R6/lib/X11/fonts ,
.Pa /usr/local/share/fonts ,
.Pa ~/.fonts ,
.Pa ~/.local/share/fonts
and
.Pa ~/.cache/fontconfig
.Pq read-only, only those that exist .
Needed for lazy font fallback after the lock.
.El
.Pp
The steady-state
.Xr pledge 2
set is derived from the enabled widgets:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dq stdio rpath
Basic I/O, timing, the already-open X11 connection and IPC, and
font file access bounded by the unveil policy.
.It Dq vminfo
Added when the memory widget is enabled because
.Dv VM_UVMEXP
requires it.
.It Dq route
Added when the network or VPN widget is enabled because
.Xr getifaddrs 3
uses
.Dv NET_RT_IFLIST .
.El
.Pp
No pledge promise permits
.Dv APM_IOC_GETPOWER .
When the battery widget is enabled and
.Pa /dev/apm
exists, the display process therefore remains unpledged and prints a
warning; its unveil policy is still locked.
.Ss Network worker
The worker runs under
.Dq stdio inet dns
with filesystem visibility locked to
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
.Pa /etc/hosts
and
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem
.Pq all read-only .
The service port is numeric, so
.Pa /etc/services
is never consulted.
.Ss IPC protocol
Communication between parent and child uses a fixed-size message
structure over
.Xr socketpair 2 :
the parent sends a one-byte fetch command, and the child responds
with a bounded 65-byte frame containing a magic byte, per-family
status codes and the address strings.
The parent validates the magic byte, the status codes and both
addresses with
.Xr inet_pton 3
before display.
.Ss Network behaviour
Public addresses are fetched over HTTPS; TLS authenticates the
service, so an on-path attacker cannot substitute another address
without a valid certificate.
The response is bounded and must be exactly one syntactically valid
address of the requested family; anything else is rejected.
The endpoint is rate-limited to one request per five minutes.
.Pp
A DNS, connection or TLS failure shows
.Dq N/A
and is retried at the next interval; the bar keeps running.
If the worker fails permanently it is not restarted (restarting would
require the
.Dq proc
promise in the display process) and public addresses remain
.Dq N/A
until the bar is restarted.
.Ss Limitations
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
restrict this process; they do not make the X11 server itself a
strong isolation boundary.
The bar necessarily talks to the user's X server, whose security is
that of the X11 protocol.
.Nm
runs as the invoking user; no setuid, privilege escalation or
.Xr chroot 2
is performed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cwm 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr tls_init 3 ,
.Xr apm 4 ,
.Xr wg 4 ,
.Xr openbarrc 5 ,
.Xr X 7
